+# Marginalia Books
+
+A static website for Marginalia Books, a fictional independent bookshop in Ashford Mill.
+It is plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript with no build step, no dependencies and no external
+requests: no CDNs, web fonts, remote images, maps or analytics.
+
+## Pages
+
+- `index.html`: home, with a short pitch, staff picks, the next events and opening hours.
+- `books.html`: a 15-title catalog with a genre filter. The chosen genre lives in the URL
+  (`books.html?genre=poetry`), so it can be bookmarked and the Back button restores it.
+- `events.html`: upcoming readings, book clubs and workshops, listed in date order.
+- `visit.html`: opening hours, address, directions and a contact form. The form is a demo:
+  it validates your entries and says plainly that nothing was sent.
+
+## Viewing the site
+
+Open `index.html` in a browser. The pages use classic deferred scripts, so no server is
+needed. To serve the folder over HTTP instead, run one of:
+
+```sh
+python3 -m http.server 8080
+npx http-server -p 8080
+```
+
+Then visit http://localhost:8080/.
+
+## File layout
+
+```
+index.html, books.html, events.html, visit.html   pages (shared header, nav and footer)
+css/styles.css                                    all shared styles
+js/main.js                                        mobile navigation toggle
+js/books.js                                       book catalog data and the genre filter
+js/events.js                                      event schedule and upcoming-events logic
+js/home.js                                        staff picks and next events on the home page
+js/visit.js                                       contact form validation
+favicon.svg                                       site icon
+tests/site.test.js                                headless Chrome tests
+package.json                                      the "test" script only
+```
+
+Book and event data live in one place each, `js/books.js` and `js/events.js`. The pages
+render from them, so the markup never repeats a title.
+
+## Running the tests
+
+The tests use Node's built-in test runner and the globally installed Playwright, driving
+Google Chrome at `/usr/bin/google-chrome` in headless mode. They start their own static
+server on an ephemeral port. Node 18 or later is required.
+
+```sh
+NODE_PATH=$(npm root -g) node --test tests/*.test.js
+```
+
+`npm test` runs the same command, but `NODE_PATH` still has to be set so that `require('playwright')`
+resolves.
+
+The tests cover every page loading with no console errors or off-origin requests, the shared
+nav and footer, the 360px layout, the genre filter and URL state, the mobile menu, the event
+date logic, and the contact form's validation and demo message.
